Richard,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on lodges,
reserves and farms; we don't sell packages.

Amakhala runs several lodges and camps across one reserve, plus the
reserve and anti-poaching operation behind them. When each camp sits on
its own link, you pay for several connections and still have gaps where
rangers and field staff actually work. One owned backbone across the
property fixes both: every camp, gate and camera on the same network,
monitored, with the carrier link feeding it at a single point.

I've copied Dwain as reserve manager, since field coverage is where this
matters most. I'd like to run a desk study on Amakhala from terrain and
public infrastructure data: the usable backbone corridors between the
camps, and where the uplink should come in. It tells you what is feasible
before anyone spends money.

If that's useful, the next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you
have, what fails, what it costs you, and what an owned network would look
like. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours
either way. The one-page proposal is attached; the short version is here:
www.cttx.co.za/assessment

